course: dictionary
lesson: 1357
title: Words with more than one name
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words change from place to place. A sweet food made with sugar is called candy in the USA, sweets in the UK and Ireland, and lollies in Australia and New Zealand. Each name is correct where it is used, so the place in the text is a clue.
explain: «Medicine is not candy.» The sentence sets candy against medicine, so they are different things. Candy is a sweet food made with sugar, like a lollipop or a toffee. Medicine is something you take to get better when you are ill, and the sentence warns you not to mix them up.
explain: «Call medicine "medicine", never sweets or candy, so small children do not look for more.» The reason after the comma is the clue. Children might go looking for more if medicine is called candy, so the sentence tells you why the word matters.
fact: Candy means a sweet food made with sugar.
fact: One piece of candy is also called a candy.
fact: In the UK and Ireland, candy is called sweets; in Australia and New Zealand, it is called lollies.
fact: «Popping candy» works differently: gas is trapped inside hard sugar and pops out as the sugar melts.
fact: «Medicine should always be called medicine, not candy or sweets, so children do not look for more.» A grown-up must always give children their medicine.
exercise: «Medicine is not candy.» What is candy? | *A sweet food made with sugar, like a toffee | A medicine that makes you better when you are ill | A dangerous drug kept in a cupboard | A kind of bread
exercise: Tom in Brisbane asks for lollies. What does he want? | Cake | Medicine for a sore throat | *Candy, such as a lollipop or a toffee | Sandwiches
exercise: «Popping candy» pops in your mouth. What makes it pop? | Very loud music | Water boiling in your mouth | Air beaten into cream | *Gas trapped inside hard sugar that escapes as the sugar melts
test: «Call medicine "medicine", never sweets or candy, so small children do not look for more.» Why does the sentence say this? | *So children do not mistake medicine for candy | Because medicine is a kind of candy | To make medicine taste sweeter | To make sweets sound more important
test: Mia unwrapped a candy and popped it into her mouth. What is a candy? | A fizzy lemonade | *One piece of sweet food made with sugar | A whole bag of medicine | A sugary drink in a bottle
exam: A grown-up says, «Medicine is never candy.» What is the message? | *Medicine and candy are different, so never mix them up | Medicine is a sweet treat for after dinner | Candy should be given as medicine when you are ill | Children can share medicine like candy
